+## `deployHookUrl` is untrusted input
+
+`deployHookUrl` is a plain text field on a site, and customers are expected
+to edit it themselves — nothing about the value is ours to trust. Without a
+check, publishing would fire a POST at whatever the field holds, including a
+loopback address, an RFC 1918 address, a cloud metadata endpoint
+(`169.254.169.254`), or a non-`http(s)` scheme, from a server that has no
+authentication in front of it for those internal targets.
+
+Before every attempt — including retries, since DNS can change between them
+— `triggerDeployHook` resolves the URL's host and rejects it unless every
+resolved address is public: no loopback, RFC 1918, link-local/metadata,
+multicast, or IPv6-equivalent range, and no `localhost` / `*.internal` /
+`metadata.google.internal` hostname. It also fetches with `redirect:
+"manual"`, so a hook that responds with a redirect to an internal address
+never gets followed. This is a denylist, not an allowlist, because the
+feature is inherently "fetch whatever public webhook URL the customer's
+static host gave them" — there's no fixed set of trusted hosts to allowlist
+against, unlike a feature with a known small set of destinations. A rejected
+URL is treated the same as an exhausted retry: `deployTriggered: false`, no
+exception raised to the publish route.
+
